# 2. Do not “clone” the unsupported theme blindly

A better goal is:

> **Rebuild the design and functionality you need as your own maintainable HopeStand theme.**

Do not directly edit the unsupported theme and do not depend on it as your production foundation. Also, review its license before copying code, images, fonts, templates, or proprietary assets into a new public theme.

Instead, use it as a **design reference**:

1. Take screenshots of every page and responsive view you like.
2. List its reusable elements:
   - Header and navigation
   - Footer
   - Buttons
   - Colors
   - Typography
   - Donation calls-to-action
   - Hero sections
   - Cards
   - Testimonial sections
   - Contact forms
   - Product cards
3. Rebuild those components one at a time with your own HTML, CSS, PHP, and WordPress templates.
4. Use your own images, logo, copy, and brand assets.

WordPress themes have a defined file and folder structure, and block themes use standard templates, template parts, styles, and configuration files. Learning that structure will make your own theme easier to maintain. 

