# 8. Use plugins intentionally—not excessively

There are no universal “mandatory plugins.” What is mandatory is having the **capability** to protect, maintain, monitor, and operate the sites responsibly.

Use the fewest well-supported plugins necessary.

## Baseline capability checklist

### Both websites need

| Capability | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Backups | Lets you restore after mistakes, attacks, or failed updates. |
| Security protection | Helps reduce common attacks and brute-force login attempts. |
| Update management | Keeps WordPress, themes, and plugins current. |
| Performance/caching | Improves page speed, especially on mobile networks. |
| Image optimization | Helps reduce slow loading caused by large images. |
| SMTP/transactional email | Improves reliability of form and order emails. |
| Spam protection | Protects contact forms and checkout forms. |
| SEO basics | Lets you manage titles, descriptions, indexing, and sitemaps. |
| Analytics | Helps you understand visitors and conversions. |
| Cookie/privacy controls, as applicable | Supports transparent data practices. |

### Marketplace also needs

| Capability | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Ecommerce engine | Products, cart, checkout, orders, inventory. |
| Payment gateway | Accepts payments and processes refunds. |
| Shipping configuration | Calculates or communicates delivery options. |
| Tax configuration | Supports the store’s tax process. |
| Fraud/checkout protection | Reduces payment and spam risk. |
| Order-notification email | Keeps customers and staff informed. |

Avoid installing two plugins that do the same job—for example, two caching plugins, two SEO plugins, or multiple security plugins. That creates conflicts and makes troubleshooting harder.

