# 9. Learn WordPress theme development in the right sequence

Do not try to learn all of PHP, WordPress, CSS, JavaScript, WooCommerce, SEO, accessibility, security, payment systems, and hosting at the same time.

Learn in this order:

## Stage 1: HTML and CSS

Learn enough to build:

- Sections
- Grids
- Responsive layouts
- Navigation
- Buttons
- Cards
- Forms
- Mobile layouts

## Stage 2: WordPress theme fundamentals

Learn:

- Theme folders and files
- `style.css`
- Theme configuration
- Templates
- Template parts
- Headers and footers
- Menus
- Page templates
- WordPress loops/content display

WordPress uses a template hierarchy to determine which template is used for pages, front pages, posts, archives, and other views. 

## Stage 3: WordPress blocks

Learn to create flexible sections that you can edit through the WordPress dashboard without rewriting code every time.

Your goal should be:

> “I can create a new HopeStand page by combining approved blocks and components.”

## Stage 4: PHP and custom functionality

Learn enough PHP to:

- Register custom post types, if needed
- Register custom blocks or settings
- Add theme options carefully
- Modify WooCommerce displays
- Create small custom plugins

## Stage 5: JavaScript

Use JavaScript later for:

- Menu interactions
- Product filters
- Dynamic search
- Sliders only when necessary
- Enhanced user experience

Do not begin with advanced JavaScript animations. A fast, clear, accessible site is more valuable than an impressive but fragile one.

